From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] WIP: set the coded format again when the resolution changes

*** NOT IN patches/libva-v4l2-request/series. INCOMPLETE AND UNSAFE. ***
***
*** It fixes the HEVC case and WEDGES THE BOARD on the H.264 one, which is
*** worse than the bug it replaces: the failure needs a physical power cycle,
*** not a reboot. Do not install this without reading
*** docs/hevc-resolution-change.md.

PR #38 sets the coded format exactly once, behind a process-wide latch. A
stream that changes resolution mid-way needs it set again: ffmpeg reacts to a
new SPS by destroying the context and its surfaces and creating both at the
new size, and with the latch held the driver is never told, so the engine
keeps decoding at the old geometry until it stalls -- 26 frames of 75 on a
vector GStreamer decodes in full.

Comparing the geometry instead of latching is narrower and correct as far as
it goes. RequestDestroyContext has already streamed both queues off and
released their buffers by that point, so S_FMT is legal there, and the cached
video_format has to be dropped with it because the capture format the driver
derives is what the surface sizes come from.

WHAT IS STILL WRONG. With this applied, r01 (HEVC, 640x480 -> 320x240 ->
640x480) decodes. r02 (H.264, 1280x720 -> 320x240 -> 1280x720) takes the
whole board down -- ssh still accepts connections, no command completes, and
only a power cycle recovers it.

So per-surface V4L2 state is still not being rebuilt in step with the format,
and the H.264 path reaches something the HEVC path does not. Earlier heap
corruption blamed on this patch was probably NOT its fault: those runs hit a
device already poisoned by a previous crash ("Failed to setup decoding job:
-22" for every client, GStreamer included), which was only discovered later.
That misattribution is the reason this note exists.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/request.h b/src/request.h
index 4f82813..70d9b4f 100644
--- a/src/request.h
+++ b/src/request.h
@@ -70,6 +70,13 @@ struct request_data {
 	 * depth BEFORE capture buffers exist -- see surface.c.
 	 */
 	unsigned int coded_bit_depth;
+	/*
+	 * The coded geometry currently programmed on the OUTPUT queue. Zero
+	 * until the first surface is created. Compared rather than latched so
+	 * a mid-stream resolution change reaches the driver -- see surface.c.
+	 */
+	unsigned int coded_width;
+	unsigned int coded_height;
 };
 
 VAStatus VA_DRIVER_INIT_FUNC(VADriverContextP context);
diff --git a/src/surface.c b/src/surface.c
index b4cba1e..993495c 100644
--- a/src/surface.c
+++ b/src/surface.c
@@ -46,7 +46,6 @@
 #include "v4l2.h"
 #include "video.h"
 
-bool SET_FORMAT_OF_OUTPUT_ONCE = false;
 
 
 /*
@@ -111,30 +110,45 @@ VAStatus RequestCreateSurfaces2(VADriverContextP context, unsigned int format,
 	bool found;
 	int rc;
 
-	//////////// HACK: this portion of the code should get cleaned up.
-
-	// v4l2_set_format needs to be called BEFORE we create any buffers
-	//
-	// we originally did this for the output stream in context.c, but 
-	// RequestCreateSurfaces2 gets called multiple times before RequestCreateContext 
-	// to allocate & map buffers. this doesn't seem to work in recent kernel versions.
-	// 
-	// we declare SET_FORMAT_OF_OUTPUT_ONCE to ensure v4l2_set_format only gets called once
-	// (in the first RequestCreateSurfaces2 call BEFORE any buffers are created later on)
+	/*
+	 * The coded format must be set before any buffer exists on the queue,
+	 * and RequestCreateSurfaces2 is called repeatedly before the context
+	 * is created, so it cannot simply be set every time. The original code
+	 * solved that with a process-wide "only ever once" flag.
+	 *
+	 * ONCE IS WRONG FOR A STREAM THAT CHANGES RESOLUTION. ffmpeg reacts to
+	 * a new SPS by destroying the context and its surfaces and creating
+	 * both again at the new size; with the flag latched, the driver was
+	 * never told, kept decoding at the old geometry, and the decode died
+	 * partway with "Timeout when waiting for media request" -- 26 frames of
+	 * 75 on a vector that GStreamer decodes in full.
+	 *
+	 * Comparing the geometry instead is both narrower and correct: repeat
+	 * calls at the same size still do nothing, and a real change reaches
+	 * the driver. Setting it is legal at this point because
+	 * RequestDestroyContext has already streamed both queues off and
+	 * released their buffers, which is the state S_FMT requires.
+	 */
 	unsigned int pixelformat = driver_data->coded_pixelformat;
 	unsigned int output_type = v4l2_type_video_output(false);
 
-	if (!SET_FORMAT_OF_OUTPUT_ONCE) {
-		rc = v4l2_set_format(driver_data->video_fd, output_type, pixelformat,
-				width, height);
-		if (rc < 0) {
+	if (driver_data->coded_width != width ||
+	    driver_data->coded_height != height) {
+		rc = v4l2_set_format(driver_data->video_fd, output_type,
+				     pixelformat, width, height);
+		if (rc < 0)
 			return VA_STATUS_ERROR_OPERATION_FAILED;
-		}
 
-		SET_FORMAT_OF_OUTPUT_ONCE = true;
-	}
+		driver_data->coded_width = width;
+		driver_data->coded_height = height;
 
-	/////////// ENDHACK
+		/*
+		 * The capture format is derived by the driver from the coded
+		 * one, so the cached video_format and its sizes have to be
+		 * re-read rather than reused from the previous resolution.
+		 */
+		driver_data->video_format = NULL;
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * YUV420_10 is accepted and then served with 8-bit surfaces. cedrus
diff --git a/src/v4l2.c b/src/v4l2.c
index 3addb33..b54c945 100644
--- a/src/v4l2.c
+++ b/src/v4l2.c
@@ -363,7 +363,12 @@ int v4l2_queue_buffer(int video_fd, int request_fd, unsigned int type,
 
 	rc = ioctl(video_fd, VIDIOC_QBUF, &buffer);
 	if (rc < 0) {
-		request_log("Unable to queue buffer: %s\n", strerror(errno));
+		/* Say WHICH queue and WHICH buffer: "Invalid argument" alone
+		 * cannot distinguish a bad index from a bad size from a
+		 * request FD in the wrong state. */
+		request_log("Unable to queue buffer: %s (type %u index %u size %u planes %u request_fd %d)\n",
+			    strerror(errno), type, index, size, buffers_count,
+			    request_fd);
 		return -1;
 	}
 
